window.BOOK = [{"chapter": 1, "chapterTitle": "A Very Small Dog with Large Opinions", "paragraphs": ["Molly believed in peace, love and having a second look at anybody carrying a sandwich.", "She was a Yorkshire terrier of modest height and extravagant appearance. Her muzzle was gold, her coat was charcoal, and the hair above her ears rose in a rainbow that suggested a small firework had made itself comfortable. She wore pink round sunglasses, a rainbow bow and a tie-dye neckerchief with a little peace charm.", "People often said she was a character.", "Molly considered this an unnecessary observation. So was everybody else. Most of them simply dressed more quietly.", "On the evening her adventure began, she was walking home through the seaside park with Jim. The wind smelled of salt, wet grass and somebody else's chips. Jim stopped to answer his phone beside the old drinking fountain.", "Molly sat politely. It was a strategic politeness. She had noticed a biscuit crumb.", "Then a blue petal drifted down from the hollow ash tree.", "It landed between her paws and turned grey.", "Molly sniffed it.", "The petal smelled of rain that had forgotten how to fall.", "From somewhere inside the tree, a small voice said, “Oh, bother. We've reached the outside.”", "Molly forgot the crumb.", "This would later be recognised as the first genuinely alarming sign."]}, {"chapter": 1, "chapterTitle": "A Very Small Dog with Large Opinions", "paragraphs": ["A mouse emerged from the roots carrying a roll of leaf paper almost as large as himself.", "He had a brown coat, a green satchel and the harassed expression of somebody who had been asked for directions while being lost.", "“Can you hear me?” he asked.", "“Of course,” said Molly.", "The mouse sighed. “Good. The gate still has enough invitation left.”", "Molly looked at the ash tree. Between two roots, a pale flower was opening around an arch that had certainly not been there on her previous walk.", "“I haven't been invited,” she said.", "“You looked at the petal and wondered what was wrong. That counts, apparently. The wood is quite old-fashioned about curiosity.”", "Behind her, Jim was saying, “No, Tuesday. I said Tuesday.”", "The mouse bowed, nearly dropping his map.", "“Pip Thistledown. Apprentice pathfinder. Currently finding fewer paths than hoped.”", "“Molly. Experienced dog.”", "“I can see that.”", "“Many can't. They say little dog, as if it explains anything.”", "Pip glanced at her rainbow hair. Then he looked through the arch, where another grey petal fell.", "“I don't suppose you know anything about colours?”", "Molly tilted her head.", "“Quite a bit of personal experience.”"]}, {"chapter": 1, "chapterTitle": "A Very Small Dog with Large Opinions", "paragraphs": ["Beyond the roots lay a woodland path under a purple sky.", "There were mushrooms with windows, lanterns that appeared to be occupied by sleeping stars, and a brook winding between ferns. Some of the ferns were bright green. Others had become the colour of old dishwater.", "Molly looked back. Jim stood three paces away, phone at his ear. A gull hung above the fountain with its wings outstretched.", "Neither moved.", "“The gate folds a minute,” Pip explained. “Inside, it lasts until moonset. Outside, scarcely the length of a sniff.”", "“That's a very imprecise unit.”", "“It's a very imprecise gate.”", "Could she go home whenever she liked?", "Pip hesitated before answering, which Molly appreciated more than a cheerful lie.", "“Until moonset, yes. After that, the gate rests. It might not open here again for a year.”", "Molly considered Jim, the suspended gull and the unfinished promise of dinner.", "Then she considered the grey petal.", "“I'm going to need you to be honest with me,” she said.", "Pip nodded.", "“And if there's a cat involved, I'd like advance warning.”", "“There are several.”", "“Ah. A complicated mission.”", "She stepped through the flowering roots."]}, {"chapter": 1, "chapterTitle": "A Very Small Dog with Large Opinions", "paragraphs": ["The first thing Molly noticed in Moonflower Wood was that the colours had scents.", "Blue smelled of rain on warm stone. Yellow smelled of toast. Red smelled like running for no reason except that your legs had received some exciting news.", "Grey smelled of almost nothing.", "That was what frightened her.", "Pip unrolled his map on a flat stone. It showed a river shaped like the roots of an enormous tree. Thin coloured paths joined a central pool, and beyond it stood a tower drawn as a curling leaf.", "“The Heartwell,” he said, tapping the pool. “It sends colour through the rootwater. Every flower lends a little. Every creature carries some. It goes round.”", "“Like gossip.”", "“More useful.”", "“You haven't heard the right gossip.”", "A grey patch spread across the corner of the leaf map. One path disappeared beneath it.", "Pip rolled the map quickly, but not before Molly saw his paws shaking.", "“My aunt lives along that path,” he said.", "Molly took off her sunglasses so he could see her eyes properly.", "“All right,” she said. “Let's find out what's happening.”", "Far away, a bell made half a note.", "The other half never arrived."]}, {"chapter": 1, "chapterTitle": "A Very Small Dog with Large Opinions", "paragraphs": ["They passed a sign nailed to a willow.", "WELCOME TO MOONFLOWER WOOD.\nPLEASE LEAVE GATES AS YOU FOUND THEM.\nUNLESS YOU FOUND THEM ARGUMENTATIVE.", "Underneath, in smaller writing, somebody had added: The third gate responds well to compliments.", "Molly approved. Clear local information was important.", "Pip told her the trouble had begun during preparations for the Moonflower Revel, a festival of music, lanterns and dancing on the broad banks of the Heartwell.", "At first, only the loudest flowers had faded. Then the red leaves lost their red. The blue beetles forgot the way to the river. By afternoon, whole paths had gone pale.", "“Nobody's hurt?” Molly asked.", "“Not yet. But when a path loses its colour, the rootwater stops moving. And things that stay still for too long begin to forget where they belong.”", "Molly looked at a bee hovering above a grey foxglove. It turned in a small, uncertain circle.", "“Right,” she said. “That sounds considerably worse than bad decorating.”", "A stocky creature stepped from behind the willow with a walking stick and a moss-green scarf.", "“Pip,” he said, “why have you brought a rainbow with teeth?”", "“Help,” said Pip.", "“Ah,” said the badger. “The expensive kind.”"]}, {"chapter": 2, "chapterTitle": "The Badger Who Filed Everything", "paragraphs": ["The badger's name was Bramble Underbough, and he was the keeper of the Leaf Library.", "He had wide paws, gentle eyes and an air of disappointed tidiness. Three leaves were stuck to his scarf. Molly suspected he had labelled them.", "“I am not a rainbow,” she told him. “I'm a Yorkshire terrier.”", "“My apologies.”", "“With rainbow features.”", "“An important distinction.”", "Bramble examined the faded map. He did not announce that everything was hopeless. He asked where the greyness had first appeared, which paths still carried water and whether anybody had checked the old sluices.", "Molly liked him immediately.", "Pip said they should go straight to the Heartwell.", "“Across the Singing Bridge?” Bramble asked.", "“It was open this morning.”", "“So was my umbrella. Conditions change.”", "He took a small packet from his bag and offered them oatcakes.", "“Adventures need feeding,” he said. “The accounts tend to leave that out.”", "Molly accepted an oatcake with the solemn courtesy she usually reserved for roast chicken.", "“Do you know what's taking the colours?” she asked.", "“I know where I'd look for an answer.”", "He pointed down a path littered with silver leaves.", "“And I know we shouldn't walk it separately.”"]}, {"chapter": 2, "chapterTitle": "The Badger Who Filed Everything", "paragraphs": ["Bramble explained the rules of the wood as they went.", "“Don't promise anything to a door unless you mean it. Don't pick a moonflower before it asks. And never say always when you can say I'll try.”", "“That's remarkably good advice outside forests,” Molly said.", "“The wood doesn't own wisdom. It merely grows more mushrooms around it.”", "At a fork, a young hedgehog was trying to push a barrow across a dry channel. The wheel had slipped between two stones. Seedlings trembled in pots behind her.", "Pip glanced at the sky.", "“Moonset,” he reminded them.", "Molly looked at the barrow.", "“A minute.”", "She braced her shoulder against its side while Bramble lifted the wheel. The hedgehog pulled, and the barrow came free with a small, damp pop.", "“Fern,” said the hedgehog, breathless. “Thank you.”", "Molly asked why the seedlings were wrapped in cloth.", "“Keeping the roots wet. The channel dried up.”", "Fern pointed at a glassy crust sealing the channel farther uphill.", "It was smooth as ice and clear as a window.", "Bramble knelt beside it.", "“That,” he said quietly, “wasn't here yesterday.”", "Beneath the crust, one trapped bubble of yellow light trembled."]}, {"chapter": 2, "chapterTitle": "The Badger Who Filed Everything", "paragraphs": ["Molly tapped the glass with one claw.", "A little tune came from inside it, clear and sweet and repeated exactly. The same three notes. The same three notes again.", "Fern winced.", "“That's my watering song. I sang it this morning.”", "“Perhaps it liked it,” Pip suggested.", "“Then it could have asked for an encore.”", "Bramble held his lantern close. The yellow light inside the glass did not flow with the water beneath it. It stayed where it had been caught.", "“Stillglass,” he said.", "The word made Pip step back.", "“What does it do?” Molly asked.", "“Holds an offered thing exactly as it was. A voice. A colour. A moment.”", "“Can we break it?”", "“Possibly. But we don't know how much water is trapped behind it.”", "Molly looked at Fern's small garden below the bank. Smashing a mystery uphill from somebody's home seemed discourteous.", "They marked the crust on Pip's map.", "Fern gave them a strip of soft green binding vine for their bags.", "“It stretches before it breaks,” she said. “The roots taught it.”", "Molly let Pip tie it around the oatcake packet.", "A useful present, she thought, often looked disappointingly ordinary until the moment you needed it."]}, {"chapter": 2, "chapterTitle": "The Badger Who Filed Everything", "paragraphs": ["At the next fork, a cat slept on a signpost.", "Molly stopped.", "“Advance warning,” she said to Pip.", "“I did mention several.”", "The cat opened one amber eye. He was large, striped and arranged across the sign as if geography were a personal cushion.", "“Where are you going?”", "“The Singing Bridge,” said Bramble.", "“Not that way.”", "“Why?” Molly asked.", "“Because I'm lying on the arrow.”", "She considered barking. It was a familiar tool and generally came to paw quickly. Instead she asked whether he could move enough for them to read it.", "The cat stretched with the care of someone unfolding a royal document.", "His name was Juniper. He had come from the river, where the bridge's roots had grown pale and a section of rail had fallen.", "“Can it still be crossed?” Bramble asked.", "“Yes. With judgement.”", "“Will you show us?”", "Juniper looked at Molly's sunglasses.", "“Only if I can say those are extraordinary.”", "“They are.”", "“That wasn't permission. That was agreement.”", "She put them back on.", "“Show us the bridge, cat.”", "He led the way, tail high, pretending he had intended to help all along."]}, {"chapter": 2, "chapterTitle": "The Badger Who Filed Everything", "paragraphs": ["The Singing Bridge crossed a river broad enough to make Molly suddenly conscious of how small her paws were.", "Its woven roots usually hummed when feet touched them. Now only the near end gave a faint answering chord. Beyond the middle, the rail had slumped towards the water.", "Pip stood beside Molly.", "“I don't like heights,” he admitted.", "“I don't like admitting things in front of cats.”", "Juniper sat on a rock, politely facing the other way.", "Bramble tested the first root with his stick. It flexed, then returned.", "“Some living strength left. We'll need to use it carefully.”", "From the opposite bank came the sound of a distant crowd preparing the Revel. A drum started, faltered and stopped.", "The river beneath the bridge carried strips of colour towards the Heartwell. Here and there the strips ended against transparent ridges.", "Molly could see yellow moving around one obstruction. Blue had become a still, shining pool.", "“It isn't just losing colour,” she said. “The colours are getting stuck.”", "Bramble nodded.", "“And every blocked channel makes the next one work harder.”", "Pip looked at his map, then at the path behind them.", "“There isn't another crossing before moonset.”", "Molly swallowed.", "“Then let's make this one sensible.”"]}, {"chapter": 3, "chapterTitle": "The Bridge That Had Forgotten Its Song", "paragraphs": ["Making a bridge sensible took rather more than positive thinking.", "Bramble checked the anchor roots. Juniper found a firm place to secure a guide vine. Pip, whose small size was suddenly useful instead of inconvenient, slipped along the near rail to inspect a gap between two woven sections.", "Molly stayed on the bank and held the lantern.", "She disliked staying on the bank. It felt less adventurous than being on the bridge.", "It was also the task they actually needed.", "The surviving roots were strong enough for one traveller at a time. The damaged rail could not bear weight. A guide vine would provide balance, but not catch Bramble if he fell.", "“Then you don't cross,” Molly said.", "Bramble looked at her.", "“You can go round later. We can bring back whatever the library says.”", "“The library is on the other side.”", "“Yes. Libraries are often awkward about location.”", "Juniper tested a broad root underneath the main span.", "“There's a lower way for someone heavy. Closer to the anchors.”", "They examined it together.", "A different route. Less graceful. Safer.", "Bramble smiled at the cat.", "“I shall try not to confuse dignity with usefulness.”", "“Please don't,” Juniper said. “There's already a shortage of dignity.”"]}, {"chapter": 3, "chapterTitle": "The Bridge That Had Forgotten Its Song", "paragraphs": ["Pip crossed first.", "He clipped his map tube to the guide vine so his paws remained free. Halfway over, he stopped so suddenly Molly's heart seemed to jump up behind her sunglasses.", "“What is it?” she called.", "“Forgot how to move.”", "“That's all right. You still know how to stay.”", "Below him the river whispered against the glass obstructions.", "Molly asked him to look at the knot just ahead of his nose, not at the far bank. Then at the next knot. He moved one paw.", "Then another.", "When he reached the far side, he sat down very hard.", "“I was brave,” he said, sounding surprised.", "“You were frightened and kept making sensible choices,” Bramble replied. “It often looks the same from a distance.”", "Molly went next.", "She took off her sunglasses and tucked them into her neckerchief. The world became sharper and considerably higher.", "At the middle, a weak root gave a soft crack.", "She froze.", "The bow above her ears trembled in the river breeze.", "“Molly?” Pip called.", "“Having a thoughtful moment.”", "She lowered her body, spread her weight and moved towards the stronger weave Bramble had marked.", "One small paw at a time."]}, {"chapter": 3, "chapterTitle": "The Bridge That Had Forgotten Its Song", "paragraphs": ["On the far bank, Molly licked Pip's ear without asking.", "He objected on principle and leaned into it in practice.", "Bramble crossed by the lower roots, moving slowly. Juniper followed along the guide line with an elegance that Molly found both impressive and mildly offensive.", "Then a grey patch spread across the near bank.", "The root they had tied the guide vine to shivered. Its bark became pale. A low chord sounded, stopped, and left the bridge silent.", "They were all safely across.", "They could no longer go back that way.", "Pip unrolled his map. The bridge line had faded to a thin white scar.", "Molly felt a cold tightening beneath her neckerchief. The gate to Jim lay beyond the river.", "“There are other paths,” Bramble said. “But we'll have to find one that's still open.”", "Juniper examined the water.", "“I'll go along the bank and warn the riverside houses. They shouldn't wait for the bridge to improve its attitude.”", "He touched noses with Bramble, ignored Molly with great ceremony and slipped between the reeds.", "Molly watched him go.", "“Decent cat,” she said reluctantly.", "“Most are,” said Pip.", "“I wouldn't put that on a sign. It might encourage them.”"]}, {"chapter": 3, "chapterTitle": "The Bridge That Had Forgotten Its Song", "paragraphs": ["Beyond the river, the wood grew quieter.", "Not pleasantly quiet, like a warm afternoon after a walk. It was the kind of quiet that made you notice you had stopped hearing something.", "A patch of bluebells stood with their heads closed. Two crickets moved their legs without making a sound. On a low branch, a wren opened her beak and looked astonished by the result.", "Bramble gave her a place on his scarf.", "“We're going to the library,” he said. “You can rest there.”", "Molly wanted to promise that they would put everything right. The promise rose easily, warm and bright, exactly the sort of thing a worried bird might like to hear.", "She remembered the rules of the wood.", "“We're going to try,” she said instead.", "The wren settled against Bramble's shoulder.", "Pip found a narrow channel with one purple thread still moving through it. He marked it carefully.", "“The water knows a way,” he said.", "“Then let's not step on it,” Molly replied.", "They followed it under a curtain of ivy.", "Ahead, an enormous oak rose above the other trees. Its windows glowed amber. A small brass bell hung beside the door.", "A sign read: LEAF LIBRARY. WHISPERING ENCOURAGED. NIBBLING BY APPOINTMENT."]}, {"chapter": 3, "chapterTitle": "The Bridge That Had Forgotten Its Song", "paragraphs": ["Bramble unlocked the library with a key shaped like a sycamore seed.", "Inside, the trunk opened into a room far larger than the outside of the tree had suggested. Shelves spiralled up through the branches. Ladders hung from pulleys. Drawers held seeds, songs and weather remembered by people with unusually good memories.", "Molly stood still, nose busy.", "“Books,” she said. “Dust. Apples. Badger.”", "“In order of importance?” Bramble asked.", "“In order of distance.”", "Pip lit three lanterns. The wren found a warm place above the desk.", "Bramble pulled open a drawer labelled GLASS, UNFORTUNATE USES OF.", "It was empty.", "He opened the one below, marked GLASS, SLIGHTLY LESS UNFORTUNATE USES OF.", "Also empty.", "Molly looked at him.", "“Do you lend these out?”", "“Not without a record.”", "He found the record.", "Two volumes had been borrowed three days earlier by the Keeper of the Heartwell.", "A silver scale clung to the edge of the page.", "Pip touched it lightly.", "“A moth scale.”", "Bramble's face changed.", "“Luma,” he said.", "For the first time since Molly had met him, he sounded less like a librarian and more like an old friend who had forgotten to call."]}, {"chapter": 4, "chapterTitle": "In Case of Excessive Magic", "paragraphs": ["Luma was a great silver moth, Bramble told them, older than most of the paths and considerably older than the Revel committee.", "She tended the Heartwell, opening its channels and guiding colour through the rootwater. Each year, when the moonflowers opened, she helped the wood share the light it had gathered.", "“Sounds lovely,” Molly said.", "“It is. It has also become very busy.”", "Pip shifted on his chair.", "“Last year's Revel had seventeen drums.”", "“Eighteen,” Bramble said. “One was filed as a large tambourine.”", "The library kept a message cabinet for letters waiting to be carried along the paths. Bramble opened Luma's compartment.", "Three unopened invitations fell out. Requests for brighter lanterns. A bigger stage. Another evening of music.", "Behind them lay a folded note in Luma's narrow handwriting.", "Could we leave one bank quiet this year? The wing-tremor is worse after loud sound. I should like to attend without having to hide.", "Bramble read it twice.", "“When did that arrive?” Molly asked.", "“While I was checking the festival inventories.”", "He looked at the neat columns on his desk.", "“I put it aside until there was time.”", "The library, which contained several centuries of useful advice, offered him no excuse."]}, {"chapter": 4, "chapterTitle": "In Case of Excessive Magic", "paragraphs": ["They found an older book in the repair room, waiting to have its spine mended.", "ON STILLGLASS AND THE PRESERVATION OF SMALL THINGS.", "Molly read over Bramble's arm while Pip held the pages flat.", "Stillglass could keep a fading song from disappearing, preserve a flower's last scent or hold a remembered colour for someone who could no longer see it.", "“It isn't bad magic,” Molly said.", "“No,” said Bramble. “But useful magic can be used badly.”", "A bowl of stillglass would hold only what had been freely offered to it. Break the bowl, and everything inside would return at once, whether the world had room for it or not.", "Let its contents go through living channels, and they could find their way home safely.", "Pip pointed at an illustration. A glass bowl stood above a spreading root system.", "“That looks like the Heartwell.”", "The page beside it carried a warning.", "NEVER ASK A LIVING WELL TO SURRENDER ALL DISCORD. A WELL CANNOT SORT AN ARGUMENT FROM A DIFFERENCE.", "Molly lowered her head.", "“All the colours are differences.”", "Bramble looked towards the silent wren.", "“And all songs are changes in the air.”", "Somewhere deep in the library, a seed drawer clicked shut by itself."]}, {"chapter": 4, "chapterTitle": "In Case of Excessive Magic", "paragraphs": ["Bramble found a draft of the Revel's opening words.", "May the Heartwell offer all its discord into keeping, that Moonflower Wood may know perfect peace.", "The last two words had been underlined.", "Pip looked at Molly's peace charm.", "“You like peace.”", "“I do.”", "“Is that different?”", "Molly thought about Jim singing while he washed the dishes. He never used the same tune as the radio. Sometimes he did not use the same tune as himself.", "She thought about gulls quarrelling over a chip, the postman greeting her and the neighbour's dog barking because he was lonely.", "“I like people being kind,” she said. “I don't need the whole world on mute.”", "The bowl could not have stolen the colours. But if the Keeper had spoken for the well, the well might have offered everything that differed, changed or interrupted.", "“Did she mean to do that?” Pip asked.", "“I don't think so,” Bramble said.", "Molly closed the book gently.", "“Then we should ask her.”", "He wrapped the repair cloth around its fragile spine and placed it in his bag.", "This time, he was taking the useful advice with him.", "Outside, the sky had become a deeper purple.", "Moonset was coming."]}, {"chapter": 4, "chapterTitle": "In Case of Excessive Magic", "paragraphs": ["The route from the library to the Heartwell passed through the Lantern Market.", "Usually, Bramble said, the stalls sold everything from bread to borrowed courage. Tonight several awnings were grey. A baker stared at a tray of pale rolls that smelled of nothing.", "Molly's ears went down.", "“Now it's personal.”", "A jackdaw in a red waistcoat called from a stall of glass jars.", "“Emergency colours! Guaranteed bright! Limited supply!”", "Inside the jars, little ribbons of red and blue pressed against the glass.", "Bramble stopped.", "“Where did those come from, Quill?”", "“Salvage.”", "“From the river?”", "“Unclaimed.”", "Molly looked at the stream beside the stall. It had gone almost clear.", "“Are those ordinary jars?” she asked.", "“Of course. The light stays if you keep the lid tight.”", "Pip watched a beetle climb onto a grey flower and turn uncertainly away.", "“Somebody is claiming it,” he said.", "Quill ruffled his feathers.", "“I didn't cause the problem.”", "“No,” Molly said. “But you've found a very small way to make it worse.”", "The jackdaw looked at his jars, then at the darkening stream.", "For once, he had no quick sales reply."]}, {"chapter": 4, "chapterTitle": "In Case of Excessive Magic", "paragraphs": ["Quill wanted to know what he should do instead.", "It was a fair question. He had gathered the light because it was disappearing, then hoped to sell it because that was what a stallholder knew how to do.", "Molly asked him to open one jar beside the water.", "The blue ribbon slid out like a fish waking up. It entered the stream, curved around a stone and moved towards the roots of a nearby violet.", "One flower lifted its head.", "The baker leaned over her counter.", "“Could you do the rest?”", "Quill stared at the violet.", "“I could charge for the jars,” he said slowly. “Afterwards. Empty ones. People need jars.”", "“An exciting new business model,” said Molly. “Selling the thing you actually own.”", "The jackdaw gave her a wounded look and began unscrewing lids.", "As the colours returned, he told them that Luma had shut the Heartwell's main gates at sunset. She had said she needed to think without everybody calling at once.", "“There is a small service path behind the lavender mill,” he added.", "He drew it on Pip's map without being asked for payment.", "Molly thanked him.", "“Don't sound surprised,” he muttered.", "“Then keep giving me practice.”"]}, {"chapter": 5, "chapterTitle": "The Door That Wanted an Honest Answer", "paragraphs": ["The lavender mill had become a grey tower beside a motionless wheel.", "Behind it, Quill's path ended at a round green door built into a bank. A brass knocker shaped like a snail watched them approach.", "“Business?” it asked.", "“We need to see the Keeper,” Bramble said.", "“So does everybody.”", "“It's urgent.”", "“So says everybody.”", "Molly sat down in front of it.", "“What would you like us to say?”", "“Something true that isn't meant to push me open.”", "Bramble began to introduce his official role. The knocker turned slightly away.", "Pip looked at his map.", "“I don't know whether my aunt's path is still there,” he said. “And I'm trying not to think about it.”", "The door's latch clicked once.", "Bramble lowered his bag.", "“I missed a message from a friend because I was busy arranging things for her.”", "The latch clicked again.", "Molly looked down at her paws.", "“I want to help,” she said. “But I also want to go home. I'm frightened that one will stop me doing the other.”", "For a moment the snail knocker said nothing.", "“That's properly inconvenient,” it murmured.", "Then the door opened.", "Molly stood.", "“Good lock.”", "“Terrible job,” said the snail. “Excellent conversations.”"]}, {"chapter": 5, "chapterTitle": "The Door That Wanted an Honest Answer", "paragraphs": ["The passage beyond the door smelled of cool earth.", "Roots threaded the ceiling, carrying faint pulses of light. Some ended in bright knots. Others had become stiff and transparent.", "Molly followed the moving colours with her nose. Beneath the greyness she caught traces of red running, blue rain and something green that smelled of a garden just after someone had decided to begin again.", "Pip marked each junction on the map.", "“Still finding paths?” Molly asked.", "“A few.”", "“Excellent. I may need one later.”", "They passed a narrow side channel sealed by a glass crust. Behind it, a little purple light blinked steadily.", "From the other side came three taps.", "Bramble knelt.", "“Who's there?”", "A faint voice answered, “Nettle. Water vole. I came to check the drain.”", "“Are you hurt?”", "“No. Annoyed. I've been saying drainage isn't decorative for years.”", "They could not safely remove the crust yet. Bramble gave Nettle the direction of an open air shaft and promised to return with a plan, not merely a larger stick.", "Molly stayed until she heard the vole reach the shaft.", "Useful adventures, she was discovering, involved a surprising amount of checking that people could breathe."]}, {"chapter": 5, "chapterTitle": "The Door That Wanted an Honest Answer", "paragraphs": ["The passage opened into the Heartwell chamber.", "An enormous tree grew through its centre, roots spreading around a clear glass bowl. Seven slender streams of colour ran from the roots into the glass. Within it, light turned and turned without finding a way out.", "Beside the bowl sat a silver moth as tall as Bramble.", "Her wings shone like frost on a quiet window. Their edges trembled.", "“Please,” she said, before anyone spoke. “One voice at a time.”", "Bramble set down his bag.", "“Luma. It's me.”", "“I know.”", "“May we come closer?”", "She nodded.", "Molly noticed folded blankets, a cold cup of tea and a stack of festival requests tied with blue ribbon. On the top sheet, somebody had drawn an extra row of lanterns.", "Luma looked at the dog.", "“You're very bright.”", "“I get that quite a lot.”", "“I didn't mean it unkindly.”", "“I know.”", "Molly pushed her sunglasses up onto her rainbow hair.", "“We think the bowl has misunderstood something.”", "Luma's wings folded tightly.", "“No,” she said. “I misunderstood something.”", "Around them, the trapped colours turned slowly.", "For the first time Molly heard what was inside the glass.", "Every missing song, all singing at once."]}, {"chapter": 5, "chapterTitle": "The Door That Wanted an Honest Answer", "paragraphs": ["Luma had made the stillglass bowl herself.", "After last year's Revel, the trembling in her wings had taken days to settle. She had asked for a quieter place this year, then watched the plans grow larger instead.", "“I thought I could put the troublesome sounds away,” she said. “Just for the evening. Everyone could enjoy themselves. I could stay.”", "“Did you ask the well?” Molly said.", "“I am its Keeper. It let me speak for it.”", "She had used the opening words while preparing the ceremony. The bowl had accepted the offering immediately.", "At first the chamber grew peaceful. Then the red in the roots began to fade. The brook stopped chattering. When she tried to release the offering, too much had already gathered behind it.", "“I opened one little outlet,” she said. “A whole spring storm came through. I closed it before the lower gardens flooded.”", "Bramble sat beside her.", "“I found your letter.”", "“Oh.”", "“Too late.”", "Luma looked at the requests on the floor.", "“I should have said no.”", "“Yes,” he said gently. “And I should have heard the quieter way you were already saying it.”", "Molly waited.", "Some silences, she was learning, were useful."]}, {"chapter": 5, "chapterTitle": "The Door That Wanted an Honest Answer", "paragraphs": ["They examined the bowl without touching it.", "Its surface held a thousand shifting reflections. Molly saw the library wren's song, Fern's watering tune and the yellow light from the flower beneath Quill's stall.", "Then she saw a piece of the park: Jim beside the fountain, the gull still suspended above him.", "“My way home is in there?”", "“Not yet,” Luma said. “But the gate draws its colour from this well. When the last moving light enters the glass, it will close early.”", "Pip looked at the moon through the hollow ceiling.", "They had less time than they thought.", "Molly wanted to demand that Luma open the bowl. She wanted the gate first, the rest afterwards. She disliked herself for wanting it and disliked the bowl even more.", "Instead she asked what a safe opening needed.", "“Living channels,” Luma said. “Places willing to receive what comes back. Small releases. Someone watching each branch.”", "Bramble opened the repair book.", "The instructions agreed.", "Molly looked at their little group: a tired moth, an anxious mouse, a badger with an overdue apology, and herself.", "“Right,” she said. “We're going to need more paws.”"]}, {"chapter": 6, "chapterTitle": "A Committee with Useful Feet", "paragraphs": ["Bramble did not call a grand council.", "He asked Pip to fetch Fern, Quill and anyone who knew the lower channels. Molly went with him. They left Luma with the bowl and Bramble with the book, because abandoning the person holding back a flood seemed a poor beginning.", "At the service door, the snail knocker asked how it had gone.", "“Honestly,” Molly said, “we've found a larger problem.”", "“Good. That's usually progress.”", "Pip's route through the market had already changed. A grey strip crossed the main square. A baker and two squirrels were lifting their stalls away from it.", "The jackdaw saw them coming and held up an empty jar.", "“All released. No refunds requested by the river.”", "“We need the jars,” Molly said.", "His eyes brightened.", "“For carrying water. And we need your help.”", "His eyes did something more complicated.", "Fern arrived with her barrow. Juniper appeared behind her, having warned the riverside houses and taken credit for several things he had not done.", "Pip unrolled the map on an overturned bread tray.", "Molly explained the bowl, the blocked rootwater and the need to send the colours home gently.", "Nobody cheered.", "They began asking practical questions.", "Molly found that much more encouraging."]}, {"chapter": 6, "chapterTitle": "A Committee with Useful Feet", "paragraphs": ["Nettle the water vole emerged from a drain carrying a forked stick.", "“Someone mentioned a plan.”", "“Someone did,” Molly said. “How are you?”", "“Dry, cross and qualified.”", "He drew the lower channels in a smear of flour. Three main branches fed the wood beyond the Heartwell: the gardens, the library bank and the old gate path across the Singing Bridge.", "The smaller channels could not carry everything at once. They would need watchers at each junction to open or close simple root sluices as the colours returned.", "“And the glass crusts?” Fern asked.", "“If live water reaches them from both ends, they'll soften,” Nettle said. “That's how stillglass behaves when it hasn't finished setting.”", "“So we need colour downstream to help colour come downstream,” Pip said.", "“Correct.”", "“That's rude.”", "“Drainage often is.”", "Molly thought of the opened jar at the violet. A small amount of free colour had woken a flower before the main channel worked.", "“Could we carry the first little releases in jars?”", "Nettle looked at Quill's stall.", "“Open jars. Carefully. To the far ends.”", "Quill puffed up.", "“Quality merchandise,” he said.", "“Excellent,” Molly replied. “Let's finally use it for something worth boasting about.”"]}, {"chapter": 6, "chapterTitle": "A Committee with Useful Feet", "paragraphs": ["There remained the problem of the bridge.", "Its gate-side roots had faded. Without a living connection across the river, colour might return to the nearer bank and stop. Molly's way home would remain cut off even if most of the wood recovered.", "She tried to ask about it casually.", "Nobody was fooled.", "Juniper traced the broken crossing with one claw.", "“The old root arch under the bridge still reaches both banks. It's grey, but not dead.”", "“Can it wake?”", "“With an unborrowed colour to start it,” said Nettle. “Something not already being called into that bowl.”", "They all looked at Molly.", "She looked back.", "“I was afraid we'd reach this part.”", "Her hair had come from outside. So had her sunglasses, neckerchief and bright cloth bow.", "She would not need to lose everything. One strongly coloured thing, freely given to the roots, might make a start.", "“Will it stay changed?” Molly asked.", "“Probably,” Nettle said.", "She felt the bow between her ears. Jim had tied it that morning while explaining that she looked ready for a festival.", "Molly swallowed.", "“I'd like to see the roots first.”", "“Of course,” said Fern.", "It helped enormously that nobody called the choice small."]}, {"chapter": 6, "chapterTitle": "A Committee with Useful Feet", "paragraphs": ["Back in the chamber, Bramble had found a safe way to test the bowl.", "Luma would release one narrow thread through a reed spout. Quill would catch it in an open jar, Fern would carry it to a dry flower bed and Nettle would watch the returning water.", "Before they began, Luma asked the garden whether it was ready to receive its colour.", "Molly expected the answer to be words.", "Instead, a thin root lifted gently towards the spout.", "“That's a yes,” Bramble said.", "“Useful. I was worried we'd need more paperwork.”", "Luma touched the glass with one soft antenna.", "A yellow thread slid into the jar.", "It did not look like much. Molly had seen more impressive things on Christmas trees. Then it moved and smelled of warm bread, summer pavements and a dog's perfectly timed nap.", "Fern carried it out with both paws.", "They waited.", "The stream under their feet gave a small, clear tick.", "A faint yellow line appeared in the roots.", "The test had worked.", "Luma's wings loosened a little.", "Molly grinned at her.", "“Good. Now we know at least one thing.”", "“One more than this morning,” said Bramble."]}, {"chapter": 6, "chapterTitle": "A Committee with Useful Feet", "paragraphs": ["They assigned the jobs before anybody could volunteer for everything.", "Nettle would manage the lower sluices. Fern would prepare the gardens. Quill would carry the jars and organise the market helpers. Juniper would watch the bridge approach. Pip would guide Molly to the old root arch, then return with news.", "Bramble would stay with Luma and help her read the release instructions. He looked unhappy about letting Molly go.", "“You've got a job here,” she told him.", "“I know.”", "“Very librarian-shaped.”", "He smiled.", "Luma asked everybody to use the old seed bells for messages. One ring meant ready. Two meant stop the release. Three meant help was needed.", "“No frantic ringing,” she added.", "“Agreed,” said Nettle. “We can be specific while alarmed.”", "Molly checked that Pip had eaten. Pip checked that she had put her sunglasses somewhere secure. Fern tied a coil of stretching vine to Molly's neckerchief.", "Then the wren from the library fluttered into the chamber.", "She opened her beak.", "One small note emerged.", "Everyone fell silent.", "The wren looked as surprised as anyone.", "“Lovely,” Molly said softly.", "It was not the whole song.", "It was enough to make beginning feel possible."]}, {"chapter": 7, "chapterTitle": "A Ribbon Across the River", "paragraphs": ["The way to the bridge ran beside a field of moonflowers.", "Their petals were closing too early. With each closing flower, the purple sky seemed to lose a little of its depth.", "Pip walked close to Molly's shoulder.", "“Do you wish you hadn't come?”", "She considered the question. A quick no would make him feel better. It would also be untrue.", "“Sometimes,” she said. “Mostly when I think about Jim looking for me.”", "Pip's ears went down.", "“But I don't wish I'd left you standing at the tree.”", "He nodded, understanding the difference.", "At the bridge, Juniper waited beside the low root arch. It curved across the river just above the water, sturdy and colourless.", "Molly crouched and touched it with her nose.", "Nothing.", "She removed her bow.", "Without it, the rainbow hair above her ears fell in an untidy fringe. The evening felt oddly cooler.", "“Would you like this?” she asked the root.", "A tiny fibre stirred beneath the grey bark.", "Juniper looked away with unusual tact.", "Molly placed the bow in a fork of the root and tied it with Fern's vine.", "“I would like to go home,” she added. “But this isn't a demand.”", "The root warmed beneath her paw."]}, {"chapter": 7, "chapterTitle": "A Ribbon Across the River", "paragraphs": ["Colour left the bow slowly.", "First the green faded, travelling through the bark in a thin, bright line. Then blue, yellow and red followed it. The woven cloth became pale cream.", "Molly watched until there was no rainbow left.", "She had expected the giving to feel wonderful.", "Mostly, it felt like missing her bow.", "Pip rested one paw against her leg.", "“You can be pleased you gave it and sorry it's gone,” he said.", "Molly looked at him.", "“Been reading library books?”", "“Sometimes the useful bits.”", "Under the bridge, the new colour moved along the arch. A little green appeared on the far bank. A shoot lifted, unfolded two leaves and held still.", "Then the light stopped.", "Molly's stomach sank.", "Nettle had warned them that starting the root was not the same as opening the channel. Somewhere underneath, a dry connection still needed water.", "Juniper peered over the bank.", "“There's the old catch-basin.”", "It lay on a ledge below them, partly filled with stones.", "“We need to clear it,” he said.", "Molly looked at the drop, the loose stones and her four small paws.", "“All right. How do we do that without falling in?”"]}, {"chapter": 7, "chapterTitle": "A Ribbon Across the River", "paragraphs": ["They did not lower Molly over the edge.", "She was small enough, which made the idea attractive, and not especially experienced at dangling, which made it a bad one.", "Instead Juniper found a sloping access path hidden behind the reeds. It was muddy and narrow, but it reached the ledge without requiring anybody to become a suspended example.", "Molly stayed at the top as a lookout while Pip marked the firm places. Juniper went down with the stretching vine secured around his middle.", "A stone shifted beneath him.", "Molly felt the vine tighten through her paws.", "“Stop,” she called.", "Juniper stopped.", "For three breaths nobody moved.", "Then he transferred his weight to a broad root and let the loose stone fall into the water.", "Pip's whiskers quivered.", "“Still all right?”", "“Yes,” the cat said. “Although I shall later describe that with more dignity.”", "He cleared the catch-basin one small stone at a time. Water seeped in around the edge, touched the pale root and began to move.", "Up on the bank, the seed bell rang once.", "Ready.", "Molly had never thought a single dry little note could sound so splendid."]}, {"chapter": 7, "chapterTitle": "A Ribbon Across the River", "paragraphs": ["Before Juniper could climb back, the Heartwell sent its first controlled release.", "A thread of purple water reached the catch-basin and curled around his paws.", "The root arch shivered.", "Molly pulled the vine gently, helping him reach the broad step. Pip held the loose coil away from the snagging reeds.", "Then a second pulse arrived, stronger than the first.", "Not a flood. Enough to make the muddy access path begin sliding.", "“Two rings,” Molly said.", "Pip ran to the bell.", "Stop. Stop.", "The flow slowed.", "Juniper climbed the last step and lay on the bank, wet to the elbows and deeply offended by mud.", "Nobody laughed until he did.", "“I have improved the river,” he announced. “It should be grateful.”", "Molly checked his paws. A shallow scrape needed cleaning, but he could walk.", "Pip wrote the problem on a leaf for Quill to carry back: pulses too close together; catch-basin needs time to drain.", "The jackdaw arrived just as the leaf was ready, which pleased him enormously.", "“Efficient transport,” he said.", "“Then transport it,” Juniper replied.", "The cat's voice was sharp, but his tail curled around the tiny mouse to keep him away from the edge."]}, {"chapter": 7, "chapterTitle": "A Ribbon Across the River", "paragraphs": ["They waited while Bramble and Luma adjusted the release.", "This time, a narrow flow reached the basin and remained narrow. Purple light travelled along the root arch, spread into the broken weave above it and lifted a section of the bridge rail.", "The bridge did not repair itself completely.", "It did something more modest and more useful: it became alive enough to grow.", "Leaves unfolded along the far bank. The old gate path acquired a faint blue edge.", "Molly smelled the park through it. Salt. Wet grass. Somebody else's chips, now at the edge of memory.", "Pip saw her lift her head.", "“You can go.”", "“Across that?”", "“Soon. The lower path is waking.”", "She wanted to run towards the scent. Every ordinary part of her wanted it.", "From the Heartwell came three faint rings.", "Help.", "The sound travelled along the revived roots, small and unmistakable.", "Molly closed her eyes for a moment.", "Then she turned.", "Juniper nodded towards the gate path.", "“I'll keep this end open as long as I can.”", "“No promises about always?” she said.", "“Not even about supper.”", "Molly touched his nose.", "He looked startled.", "“Decent dog,” he muttered as she ran."]}, {"chapter": 8, "chapterTitle": "The Storm in the Bowl", "paragraphs": ["At the Heartwell, the glass had begun to sing.", "Not the gentle single note of the wren. A deep, crowded vibration filled the chamber, and the coloured streams entering the bowl had thickened.", "Luma stood with her wings pressed close. Bramble held the release book open, but several pages were rattling too hard to read.", "“What changed?” Molly asked.", "“The wood is waking,” he said. “More roots are offering their discord because the original invitation is still in force.”", "They had made outlets. They had not stopped the bowl taking things in.", "Molly stared at the old opening words on the floor.", "Perfect peace.", "The phrase now sounded less like a wish and more like a very badly arranged cupboard.", "Luma tried to withdraw the offering again. The bowl answered with a bright pulse that sent water climbing the walls of its roots.", "The incoming flow exceeded the safe releases.", "“Close the upper channels?” Pip asked.", "“That would push it into the gardens,” Nettle said, arriving through a drain. “The pressure needs somewhere to go.”", "Molly listened beneath the crowded singing.", "One low sound repeated under everything else.", "Not angry. Not threatening.", "A great tree trying to finish a sentence."]}, {"chapter": 8, "chapterTitle": "The Storm in the Bowl", "paragraphs": ["Molly asked everyone to stop speaking.", "They looked at her, then listened.", "The low sound rose from the roots supporting the bowl. She placed one ear against the bark.", "Wet earth. Old leaves. A thousand seasons of things arriving, changing and leaving again.", "Then a word, so slow it was almost a movement.", "Who?", "Molly lifted her head.", "“The well's asking who's speaking for it.”", "Luma's antennae drooped.", "“I am.”", "“Is that still what you want?”", "“No.”", "“Have you told it that part?”", "Luma opened her mouth, then closed it.", "She had ordered the bowl to release. She had argued with the offering. She had not given up the assumption that she must decide alone what peace should mean for the wood.", "Bramble lowered the book.", "“The Keeper tends the well,” he said softly. “She doesn't own it.”", "Luma looked at the folded invitations. The unfinished requests. The shining thing she had made when she was too tired to ask anybody to stop.", "“I thought if I were good at the job, I wouldn't need help.”", "Molly sat beside her.", "“I'm an excellent dog,” she said. “I still require someone to open the food.”", "Luma gave one surprised, shaky laugh."]}, {"chapter": 8, "chapterTitle": "The Storm in the Bowl", "paragraphs": ["Luma touched the tree with both antennae.", "“I cannot speak for every root,” she said. “I withdraw my keeping of what was not mine to silence.”", "The glass bowl became perfectly clear.", "For one hopeful moment, Molly thought the problem was solved.", "Then everything inside it moved towards the outlets at once.", "Bramble caught the reed spout before it tore loose. Nettle braced the lower sluice. Pip rang two urgent, separate notes.", "Stop the release.", "But the bowl no longer recognised Luma's right to hold it shut.", "Molly ducked beneath a spray of blue water.", "There was the storm Luma had feared, gathering itself to become everybody else's problem.", "“We need a temporary holding place,” Bramble said.", "“Not another bowl,” Molly replied.", "“The stillglass can hold it briefly if something freely offered makes a new small boundary.”", "A willing song. A remembered moment. Something with a clear beginning and end.", "Molly thought of the wren's single note.", "Too short.", "She thought of her bow.", "Already given.", "Then she thought of the longest, warmest sound in her own small world.", "Jim calling her home.", "She took one step towards the glass.", "Then stopped to ask the question that mattered.", "“What would it take from me?”"]}, {"chapter": 8, "chapterTitle": "The Storm in the Bowl", "paragraphs": ["Bramble read the warning aloud.", "A memory placed in keeping would not be destroyed. But until released, it would feel distant to the one who had offered it.", "Molly would know there had been a voice. She might not be able to hear it inside herself.", "Luma shook her head.", "“You shouldn't have to.”", "“No,” said Molly. “I shouldn't. Can anyone offer something else?”", "Pip offered his aunt's song. Fern offered the rhythm she used to sow seeds. Bramble offered a poem he remembered from his mother.", "Together, they might make a boundary long enough for the watchers to reopen the channels properly.", "Molly looked at them.", "It had not occurred to her that the important part could be shared.", "“I'll offer my call,” she said. “Alongside yours. Only until the next quiet bell.”", "They stated the limit carefully. Luma repeated it so the well could hear.", "Not all discord. Not perfect anything.", "Four small memories, freely lent for one task.", "The bowl warmed.", "Molly heard Jim's voice once, close and bright.", "Then it moved away behind the glass.", "The rising water steadied.", "Pip pressed against her leg.", "“Still know who you are?” he whispered.", "“Molly,” she said.", "“Good.”", "“And hungry.”", "“Very good.”"]}, {"chapter": 8, "chapterTitle": "The Storm in the Bowl", "paragraphs": ["With the storm held for a little while, the chamber became busy in a better way.", "Nettle went to the sluices. Fern ran to the garden branch. Quill carried the updated plan to every watcher he could find, repeating each message back before taking off.", "Bramble and Luma worked side by side at the reed spouts. She felt the pressure. He read the marks. Neither pretended to know what the other knew.", "Molly stayed where the tree's slow voice was clearest.", "The roots asked their questions in movements and scents. Ready? Room? More?", "She relayed them to Pip, who rang the appropriate bell.", "A little yellow went to the gardens. They waited for the return signal.", "A little blue went to the library. They waited again.", "Purple reached the bridge branch. Its watcher rang once.", "The system was slow, inconvenient and full of pauses.", "It worked.", "Then a cold scent entered the chamber.", "Grey, but different from before. This time it smelled of rain clouds, evening stone and the soft underside of a moth's wing.", "Nettle's voice came from the lower passage.", "“The grey's backing up. Someone's shut its channel.”", "Molly looked at the turning glass.", "They had remembered every bright colour.", "They had forgotten one shade of the wood itself."]}, {"chapter": 9, "chapterTitle": "A Place for the Grey", "paragraphs": ["At the lower channel stood two young squirrels and a shrew with a clipboard.", "They had blocked the grey outlet with a plank.", "“We thought it was the problem coming back,” the shrew explained when Molly arrived.", "The water behind the plank pressed upwards, carrying silver, charcoal and soft cloud-colours. None smelled empty.", "Molly looked at the squirrels' grey winter tails, Nettle's dark paws and the shadows beneath the roots.", "“Was everything grey before because there was too much grey?” she asked.", "Nettle shook his head.", "“Because all the living colour was being held. Empty things only looked grey.”", "The shrew lowered the clipboard.", "“Oh.”", "Molly nodded towards a little shadow underneath the plank.", "“There's a difference between a quiet place and a place where nothing can speak. Apparently there's a difference with grey, too.”", "They lifted the plank together.", "Silver water flowed into the channel. Shadows returned beneath the ferns. A white flower acquired the soft grey edges that made its petals look wonderfully real.", "The roots stopped straining.", "Nettle examined the water level and gave a satisfied grunt.", "“Much better.”", "The shrew crossed out BAD WATER on the clipboard.", "Underneath, in smaller writing, she wrote: ASK FIRST."]}, {"chapter": 9, "chapterTitle": "A Place for the Grey", "paragraphs": ["When Molly returned, Luma was watching the grey stream with tears in her large dark eyes.", "“I thought they wouldn't want any of my quiet back.”", "Bramble put down the measuring reed.", "“Some of us were foolish. That doesn't mean you have to disappear.”", "Luma's wings loosened. Their trembling had eased, though it had not gone away.", "The wood could recover without her becoming somebody different.", "Molly was beginning to think that might be the point of quite a few things.", "Above them, moonlight narrowed in the hollow ceiling. Pip checked his map.", "The gate path was blue again. At its far end, a tiny flower-symbol blinked weakly.", "“The fold is nearly finished,” he said.", "Molly reached for Jim's voice inside herself and found the distant place where it waited.", "It frightened her more than she expected.", "Bramble saw.", "“The quiet bell comes next. We release the four memories with the last stream.”", "“How long?”", "“A few more careful moments.”", "Molly stared at the fading flower on the map.", "She wanted somebody to promise there was enough time.", "Nobody did.", "Instead Pip began rolling his map, ready to guide her at the moment the work was done."]}, {"chapter": 9, "chapterTitle": "A Place for the Grey", "paragraphs": ["The last colour inside the bowl was not a bright ribbon.", "It was the blue-grey of morning before the first bird decided it was worth singing. It moved slowly around the four borrowed memories, unwilling to find an outlet.", "“What does it need?” Bramble asked.", "Molly put her ear to the tree.", "Rest, the roots answered.", "She looked at the repaired channels. Every watcher was ready to welcome something back. Gardens wanted sunlight. The library wanted voices. The market wanted its warm, noisy evening.", "Who had offered to receive rest?", "Luma lifted her head.", "“The east bank,” she said. “My old quiet place.”", "It had been included in the plans for the bigger festival stage. Someone would have to move the ropes, take down the markers and leave the ground unclaimed.", "Quill was already spreading his wings.", "“East bank. No stage. Make room for the quiet.”", "“Repeat it back,” Bramble said automatically.", "“I just did.”", "“Quite right. Carry on.”", "The jackdaw left like a thrown shadow.", "Molly watched the final light circling.", "It was not refusing to belong.", "It was waiting for somewhere it could belong without having to shout."]}, {"chapter": 9, "chapterTitle": "A Place for the Grey", "paragraphs": ["One bell rang from the east bank.", "Ready.", "Luma guided the last stream towards the newly opened root. The blue-grey light slipped through, slow and soft. Somewhere outside, a breeze passed through reeds without rattling them.", "The bowl emptied.", "For a heartbeat it held only four small lights.", "Pip's aunt singing. Fern counting seeds. Bramble's mother halfway through a poem.", "And Jim.", "Luma touched the glass.", "“The keeping is finished,” she said.", "The lights rose together, separated and went home.", "Molly heard her name.", "Not from the chamber. Not as a noise beside her ears. She heard it in the place where a loved voice belonged, with all its ordinary warmth restored.", "“Molly. Come on, love.”", "Her tail began wagging before she could make any dignified decision about it.", "Pip laughed, then blinked very fast as his own song returned.", "The empty bowl settled into its roots. Without its trapped light, it looked quite ordinary.", "Luma exhaled.", "Then the flower-symbol on the map lost one of its petals.", "Molly turned towards the service passage.", "Bramble picked up his lantern.", "“Go,” he said.", "“I haven't thanked everyone.”", "“Write later.”", "For once, the librarian recommended postponing the words."]}, {"chapter": 9, "chapterTitle": "A Place for the Grey", "paragraphs": ["Molly and Pip ran through the service passage.", "The snail knocker called something about excellent progress. Molly did not stop to ask whether it was being sarcastic.", "The market had colour again. Red awnings. Blue jars. A baker carrying rolls that smelled so wonderful Molly suffered a brief and heroic crisis of priorities.", "“Later!” she called.", "The baker tossed an oatcake into Pip's open map tube.", "They reached the bridge as the last moonflowers on the bank began folding shut.", "Juniper waited at the lower path. His cleaned paw rested on a broad root.", "“Steady crossing,” he said. “Running is for the bits on land.”", "Molly obeyed.", "The revived arch carried them above the river, its surface rough and sure beneath her paws. At the far bank, the pale cloth bow still hung where she had tied it.", "She stopped.", "The root lifted the bow towards her.", "It had kept the colour she offered. It did not need to keep the thing itself.", "Molly tucked the cream bow into her neckerchief.", "Then she followed Pip down the blue-edged path.", "Ahead, the old ash roots framed a narrowing patch of seaside park.", "Jim stood beside the fountain.", "The gull had begun to move."]}, {"chapter": 10, "chapterTitle": "Not Quite the Same Bow", "paragraphs": ["Molly reached the gate with Pip beside her.", "On the other side, Jim was lowering his phone. The folded minute was unfolding, every outside second stretching towards its proper size.", "“I can't come through unless the tree invites me,” Pip said.", "“I know.”", "“And it doesn't always open in the same place.”", "“I know.”", "Neither of them said goodbye immediately.", "The wood behind Pip glowed with returning colour. Yellow along the roots. Purple in the flowers. Grey beneath the leaves, making the bright parts brighter.", "Molly touched his nose with hers.", "“Find your aunt's path.”", "“I will.”", "“And eat the oatcake.”", "“I was saving it.”", "“For what?”", "He considered the last hour.", "“Good point.”", "He broke it and offered her half.", "Molly accepted.", "Some ceremonies did not need a speech.", "The gate narrowed another inch. Pip stepped back.", "Molly slipped through the roots and onto the park grass.", "Behind her, the flowers folded into the ash tree with a sound like somebody closing a book very carefully.", "Jim looked down.", "“There you are. Been investigating?”", "Molly sat at his feet, cream bow tucked against her peace charm.", "He had no idea.", "She wagged anyway."]}, {"chapter": 10, "chapterTitle": "Not Quite the Same Bow", "paragraphs": ["On the walk home, Jim noticed the missing bow.", "“Well,” he said, “you've certainly done something with yourself.”", "Molly looked innocent.", "This was a useful expression, though difficult to maintain with rainbow hair full of woodland leaves.", "At home he found the pale cloth in her neckerchief. He turned it over, puzzled, then checked it for mud.", "“Didn't know this one changed colour.”", "Neither had Molly, until circumstances became educational.", "He washed the bow gently and laid it by the kitchen window. Then he gave her dinner.", "For several minutes the problems of magical woodland management could wait.", "Afterwards Molly went to the back door and looked into the dark garden.", "An ordinary moth moved around the porch light. It was small and brown and plainly did not need advice about a Heartwell.", "Molly asked it to say hello to Luma anyway.", "The moth continued being a moth.", "“Fair enough,” she said.", "Inside, Jim began washing the dishes. He sang the wrong words to the right tune, then the right words to a completely different tune.", "Molly lay down where she could hear him.", "She no longer mistook the familiar for something that would always be there without anybody looking after it."]}, {"chapter": 10, "chapterTitle": "Not Quite the Same Bow", "paragraphs": ["In Moonflower Wood, the Revel did not take place as planned.", "The big stage moved away from the east bank. The drummers agreed on a shorter set and an actual finishing time. A sign marked the quiet path, with no apology underneath it.", "Luma came for a little while.", "When her wings began trembling, she left with Fern to sit among the reeds. Nobody announced that she was missing the best part. From the quiet bank, she could see the lanterns reflected in the water.", "Bramble read his mother's poem to three mice, a sleepy beetle and a squirrel who claimed to be listening with his eyes closed.", "Quill sold clean empty jars and labels. His stall did surprisingly well.", "Nettle received a public thank-you for the drains, which embarrassed him so much he inspected a drain during it.", "Juniper lay on a cushion near the repaired bridge and allowed people to admire his bandaged paw.", "Pip found his aunt's path shining brown and gold beneath the hazel trees.", "She hugged him until he squeaked.", "“Where have you been?” she asked.", "“Finding a way.”", "Then he ate something warm at her small table and told her about a very bright dog who had learned that bravery could ask for help."]}, {"chapter": 10, "chapterTitle": "Not Quite the Same Bow", "paragraphs": ["Three evenings later, Molly met a grey old dog outside the corner shop.", "He usually barked when she passed. Molly usually barked back. They had maintained the arrangement for some time without either appearing particularly satisfied by it.", "Tonight she stopped a little farther away.", "The old dog looked at her cream bow, now tied neatly above her rainbow fringe.", "“Something happened to your hat.”", "“It's a bow.”", "“Something happened to it.”", "“Yes.”", "He waited. Dogs were better at waiting than people sometimes remembered.", "“I gave its colours away,” Molly said.", "“Why?”", "“Someone needed them.”", "The old dog considered this.", "“Are you upset?”", "“A bit. Also pleased.”", "“Sounds tiring.”", "“It was.”", "For a while they watched a pigeon make a series of poor decisions near a paper bag.", "Then the old dog shifted, making room for her beside the low wall.", "Molly sat down.", "Jim, waiting by the shop door, looked astonished by the absence of barking.", "“Making friends?” he asked.", "Molly did not answer in any way he could understand.", "She was busy discovering that not every quiet beginning needed to become a conversation immediately.", "The pigeon found a crumb.", "Everybody agreed it had worked hard enough to deserve it."]}, {"chapter": 10, "chapterTitle": "Not Quite the Same Bow", "paragraphs": ["A week later, a leaf appeared beside Molly's bed.", "It was green on one side and silver on the other, folded into a shape that might have been an envelope if envelopes had grown whiskers.", "Inside was a map.", "Not the whole of Moonflower Wood. Just the path from the ash tree to the Heartwell, drawn by a careful mouse who was improving at his work.", "Three small pictures marked the way: a library, a bridge and a quiet bank beneath a great silver moth.", "At the bottom, in Bramble's precise handwriting, were the words:", "The wood is well. Your place remains your own.", "Pip had added: No adventure currently scheduled. Probably.", "And underneath, in a sprawling line that could only belong to Juniper:", "Bring a towel. Some of us remember the river.", "Molly laughed softly.", "She put the map beneath her bed, beside a favourite ball and a perfectly respectable stick. Then she adjusted her cream bow in the reflection from the window.", "Her rainbow hair stuck out in every direction.", "Her sunglasses were slightly crooked.", "She looked, she decided, wonderfully herself.", "From the kitchen came Jim's voice.", "“Molly? Walk?”", "Her tail answered first.", "Peace, love and sensible footwear, she thought.", "Then she ran to the door.", "THE END"]}];
